<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <title>Text Adventure</title>
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
    <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
    <%# Still one inline <style>: propshaft is here to serve JavaScript, and the
        reading experience is a stage of its own (`ta-api-iface`). Restyling
        anything is that task's job, not this file's. %>
    <style>
      :root { color-scheme: dark; }
      body {
        background: #14161a; color: #cfd3d8;
        font: 15px/1.6 ui-monospace, "DejaVu Sans Mono", monospace;
        margin: 0; padding: 2rem 1rem;
      }
      main { max-width: 44rem; margin: 0 auto; }
      h1 { font-size: 1.2rem; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: #8ba0b4; }
      h2 { font-size: 1rem; color: #e6e9ec; margin: 0 0 .25rem; }
      a { color: #7fb2d8; }
      hr { border: 0; border-top: 1px solid #262b31; margin: 1.5rem 0; }
      .preface { color: #9aa3ac; font-style: italic; white-space: pre-wrap; }
      .turn { white-space: pre-wrap; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; }

      /* The log dims every turn but the newest, so a player who has scrolled
         can find where the turn they just took begins. Past turns take the
         preface's colour; the newest keeps full-strength body colour. Colour
         only -- the preface is italic as well, and a turn is not.
         `:last-of-type` is the marker: no class per entry, and on a brand new
         playthrough the opening scene is the only turn and so the newest one.
         While a turn is streaming the newest turn is the #stream div *below*
         the log, so the log carries `streaming` and nothing in it brightens.
         PlaythroughsControllerTest asserts these two selectors directly. */
      .log .turn { color: #9aa3ac; }
      .log:not(.streaming) > .turn:last-of-type { color: #cfd3d8; }
      .command { color: #d8c07f; margin: 0 0 .35rem; }
      .speaking { color: #8b939b; margin: 0 0 .35rem; }
      .place { color: #8b939b; margin: 0 0 .75rem; }
      .place strong { color: #cfd3d8; font-weight: normal; }
      .story { border: 1px solid #262b31; padding: .75rem 1rem; margin-bottom: .75rem; }
      .story p { color: #8b939b; margin: .25rem 0 .75rem; }
      form { display: flex; gap: .5rem; }
      input[type=text] {
        flex: 1; background: #0e1013; color: #cfd3d8; border: 1px solid #333b44;
        padding: .5rem .6rem; font: inherit;
      }
      button {
        background: #262b31; color: #cfd3d8; border: 1px solid #3a424b;
        padding: .5rem .9rem; font: inherit; cursor: pointer;
      }
      button:hover { background: #333b44; }
      .cursor::after { content: "\2588"; animation: blink 1s steps(2) infinite; }
      @keyframes blink { 50% { opacity: 0; } }
      .empty { color: #6b737b; }
      .alert { color: #d88f7f; border: 1px solid #4a3230; padding: .5rem .75rem; }

      /* The app talking rather than the world -- see Playthrough::SafetyNotice.
         Deliberately not `.alert`: nothing has gone wrong and the alarm colour
         would say it had. A left rule and the preface's grey put it plainly
         outside the fiction without shouting, and `.turn`'s pre-wrap is absent
         on purpose because this text is the app's own paragraphs rather than a
         model's. It sits at the foot of the log, where the turn would have
         been. */
      .notice {
        border-left: 2px solid #4a5a4a; padding: .1rem 0 .1rem .75rem;
        margin: 0 0 1.5rem; color: #9aa3ac;
      }
      .notice p { margin: .5rem 0; }
      .notice strong { color: #cfd3d8; font-weight: normal; }
    </style>
    <%= javascript_importmap_tags %>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <% if flash[:alert] %><p class="alert"><%= flash[:alert] %></p><% end %>
      <%= yield %>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
